<div class="page">

  <header class="hero">
    <div class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Field report &mdash; 14 August 2026</div>
    <h1>Callisto</h1>
    <p class="tagline">The fast, crash-safe, polyglot monorepo release engine. Native Rust, replacing Node-based changeset tooling, regex manifest edits, and hand-maintained CI matrices.</p>

    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">10</div><div class="label">crates</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">810+</div><div class="label">tests passing</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">168</div><div class="label">commits this week</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">12</div><div class="label">fixes shipped today</div></div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section id="what">
    <div class="eyebrow"><h2>What it does</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">Callisto discovers a Cargo / npm / PyPI monorepo's package graph, resolves version bumps from changesets across the dependency DAG, and applies them &mdash; manifests, changelogs, lockfiles, tags, publishes &mdash; without ever touching a file it can't roll back.</p>
    <div class="cap-grid">
      <div class="cap">
        <div class="cap-title">Format-preserving edits</div>
        <div class="cap-body">Manifests are edited via CST (<code style="font-family:var(--mono)">toml_edit</code>, JSON indentation fingerprinting), not regex. Comments, ordering, and whitespace survive every bump.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <div class="cap-title">Crash-safe writes</div>
        <div class="cap-body">Every write lands via temp-file-then-atomic-rename. A killed process never leaves a half-written manifest behind.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <div class="cap-title">Topological cascades</div>
        <div class="cap-body">A <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">petgraph</code> DAG with Tarjan SCC detection computes cascading bumps across the whole workspace and catches circular dependencies before they ship.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <div class="cap-title">Zero-config CI matrices</div>
        <div class="cap-body"><code style="font-family:var(--mono)">callisto matrix</code> reads napi-rs and maturin native targets straight from manifests &mdash; one source of truth instead of hand-maintained YAML.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="week">
    <div class="eyebrow"><h2>This week</h2></div>
    <div class="timeline">

      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-date">Aug 8</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Foundation hardening</div>
        <div class="tl-body">Decoupled <code>callisto-conventional</code> from the VCS layer, fixed a workspace-inherited-version overwrite bug, closed a shell-injection hole in the CI action, and taught publish to detect the right package manager (pnpm / yarn / bun / npm) automatically.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-date">Aug 11&ndash;12</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Shipped <code>callisto matrix</code></div>
        <div class="tl-body">A flagship feature built end to end in two days: native build target auto-discovery for napi-rs and maturin, runtime-version constraints, CLI surface, and CI wiring &mdash; 24 commits, full spec-to-implementation cycle.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-date">Aug 12</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Full workspace audit</div>
        <div class="tl-body">24-agent adversarial sweep across architecture, security, performance, and correctness. Found and fixed 8 confirmed critical/high bugs same-day, including a version parser that crashed on this repo's own <code>version.workspace = true</code> crates, a silent no-op in NuGet/GitHub Release publishing, and a path-traversal write via an unvalidated changelog config path.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-date">Aug 13</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Manifest write performance, done properly</div>
        <div class="tl-body">Two specs, both through full adversarial spec and plan review before a line was written: splitting manifest mutation from disk persistence, then batching same-file writes so a cascade touching one <code>Cargo.toml</code> five times opens, mutates, and persists it once instead of five.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-date">Aug 14 &mdash; today</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Re-audit and backlog sweep</div>
        <div class="tl-body">Re-verified all previously-deferred audit findings against current code with four parallel agents &mdash; several had already been fixed or partially fixed without being logged. Built a clean, priority-ordered backlog of 31 items and shipped the first 12 the same day.</div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="today">
    <div class="eyebrow"><h2>Today, in detail</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">Twelve fixes, each independently reviewed before it landed. Three were genuine security holes; the rest closed silent-failure and type-safety gaps. The review step itself caught two real bugs before they shipped.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout-label">Caught by review, not by the original fix</div>
      <p>The tag-name injection fix was reviewed by a second pass that traced a sibling code path and found tag names read back from the repository itself &mdash; not just ones Callisto renders &mdash; flowed unguarded into shelled <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">git diff</code>/<code style="font-family:var(--mono)">git log</code> calls. Same class of bug, different door. Closed in the same commit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout-label">Caught by review, not by the original fix</div>
      <p>A secret-redaction fix for subprocess stderr had a URL-parsing heuristic that <em>looked</em> right but silently failed to redact a credential when the password itself contained a <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">/</code> or a second <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">@</code> &mdash; exactly the shape a base64 token takes. Rewritten to fail toward over-redaction instead of under-redaction.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="pill-row">
      <span class="pill fixed">git tag CLI injection</span>
      <span class="pill fixed">credential leak in error logs</span>
      <span class="pill fixed">subprocess memory-exhaustion DoS</span>
      <span class="pill fixed">silent no-op on malformed manifests</span>
      <span class="pill fixed">401/403 false-positive misclassification</span>
      <span class="pill fixed">napi Linux target never resolved</span>
      <span class="pill fixed">PEP 503 name mismatch on cascades</span>
      <span class="pill fixed">duplicate CI matrix jobs</span>
      <span class="pill caught">unscoped npm package silently dropped explicit public access</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <div class="eyebrow"><h2>What's next</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">Nineteen items left in the backlog, grouped into five phases and sequenced by risk. Same bar for all of them: red test, independent review, full suite green, before anything is called done.</p>

    <div class="progress-block">
      <div class="progress-head">
        <span>Backlog</span>
        <span class="frac">12<span>/31</span></span>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width: 38.7%; height: 100%;"></div></div>

      <div class="tier-list">
