  <div class="hero-inner">
    <p class="hero-eyebrow"><span class="crater"></span>Field atlas &middot; polyglot release engineering</p>
    <h1 class="title">Callisto</h1>
    <p class="title-sub">v0.3.3 &middot; 10 crates &middot; unsafe_code = forbid</p>
    <p class="tagline">Named for Jupiter's most heavily cratered moon &mdash; every impact still visible, layered, in order. Which is exactly what a versioning tool has to do for a codebase.</p>

    <div class="stat-strip">
      <div class="s"><div class="n">10</div><div class="l">crates</div></div>
      <div class="s"><div class="n">47.8k</div><div class="l">lines of Rust</div></div>
      <div class="s"><div class="n">794</div><div class="l">test cases</div></div>
      <div class="s"><div class="n">14</div><div class="l">CLI commands</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="shell">
  <div class="index-wrap">
    <nav class="index" aria-label="Plate index">
      <p class="idx-title">Plates</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#problem">The coordination gap</a></li>
        <li><a href="#positioning">Prior art &amp; positioning</a></li>
        <li><a href="#origin">Origin of the name</a></li>
        <li><a href="#architecture">Layered architecture</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pipeline">How a release moves</a></li>
        <li><a href="#specimens">Feature specimens</a></li>
        <li><a href="#principles">Design principles</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cli">Command surface</a></li>
        <li><a href="#running">Where it runs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#docs">Documentation map</a></li>
        <li><a href="#scale">Scale &amp; rigor</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <main>

    <section class="plate" id="problem">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate I</p><h2 class="plate-title">The coordination gap</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Every mature polyglot monorepo hits the same wall eventually: Rust crates, npm packages, and increasingly napi-bridged packages between them &mdash; and no single existing tool understands version coordination across all three cleanly.</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Callisto's specific wedge is the <strong>napi pattern</strong>: one Rust crate compiles down to N platform-specific npm packages (<code>@pkg/darwin-arm64</code>, <code>@pkg/linux-x64-gnu</code>, and so on) plus a "main" npm package that depends on all of them via <code>optionalDependencies</code> &mdash; and every one of those packages has to move in lockstep, in one version, across two entirely different registries. Nothing in the current tooling landscape handles that natively.</p>
        <p>So it discovers the workspace's package graph across Cargo, npm, and PyPI; resolves version bumps from changesets across the dependency DAG; and applies them &mdash; manifests, changelogs, lockfiles, tags, publishes &mdash; without touching a file it can't roll back. It's a hermetic native binary, not a build-system plugin: it runs standalone in Bazel sandboxes, Buck2, Nix flakes, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and local Git hooks, with a Moon WASM extension as one integration among several &mdash; not a precondition.</p>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="spec">
        Callisto isn't trying to out-language release-please or Nx Release &mdash; that's not the fight worth picking. Its edge is sharper and more specific: teams that prefer the changesets file format over Conventional Commits, and need napi-style cross-registry coordination that nothing else handles. A specific, well-defined edge, not a claim to have out-built either incumbent on raw language coverage.
        <cite>docs/00-design.md &sect;3.2</cite>
      </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section class="plate" id="positioning">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate II</p><h2 class="plate-title">Prior art &amp; positioning</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Callisto brings together the best ideas from <code>@changesets/cli</code>, Google's <code>release-please</code>, and <code>nx release</code> &mdash; and names its debts explicitly rather than pretending to invent the category.</p>

      <div class="quote-grid">
        <div class="diffcard">
          <p class="vs">vs. <b>@changesets/cli</b></p>
          <p>JS-only. Polyglot teams patch around it today with proxy <code>package.json</code> files and hand-written sync scripts &mdash; a documented, real pattern, not a hypothetical. Re-serializes JSON with destructive default formatting.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diffcard">
          <p class="vs">vs. <b>release-please</b></p>
          <p>Relies on fragile regular expressions for manifest edits and focuses on single-repo releases. Genuinely polyglot in scope, but has no concept of napi platform-package coordination.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diffcard">
          <p class="vs">vs. <b>Nx Release</b></p>
          <p>Deeply, structurally coupled to Nx's JavaScript project graph &mdash; not something you adopt independently of the rest of Nx.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diffcard">
          <p class="vs">vs. <b>knope</b></p>
          <p>Handles Cargo + npm dual-published packages, and its "one version across manifests" model is the direct ancestor of Callisto's own &mdash; but it writes exact versions everywhere (no npm range preservation) and has no napi awareness.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="highlight-strip">
        Across a nine-axis comparison in the design doc &mdash; intent-capture format, workspace support, cross-ecosystem cascade, dual-published packages, napi coordination, library-shipping, Moon-extension support, range preservation, self-publishing &mdash; <strong>Callisto is the only one of the five with native napi platform-package coordination.</strong>
      </div>

      <p class="prose" style="margin-top:20px;">The design doc also names its own risk: the changesets project has an open issue requesting native polyglot support. If that ships, this differentiation narrows. Stated plainly, in the doc's own words &mdash; not hedged out of a pitch deck.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="plate" id="origin">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate III</p><h2 class="plate-title">Origin of the name</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Jupiter's moon Callisto is the most heavily-cratered body in the solar system &mdash; and that's the point.</p>
      <blockquote class="spec">
        Every impact over roughly four billion years is still visible, layered, in order &mdash; which is what a versioning tool preserves for a codebase.
        <cite>docs/00-design.md &sect;1, "Naming"</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="prose">At the time of writing the name was unclaimed on crates.io; the bare name on npm belongs to an unrelated, abandoned framework, so the project publishes there scoped as <code>@orin-dx/callisto</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="plate" id="architecture">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate IV</p><h2 class="plate-title">Layered architecture</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Ten crates in four strata &mdash; each layer depends only downward, the same one-directional rule that makes a crater record legible.</p>

      <div class="diagram-frame">
        <p class="fig-label">Fig. 1 &mdash; crate dependency layers (license split noted per crate)</p>
        <pre class="mermaid">
