    <section class="plate" id="pipeline">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate V</p><h2 class="plate-title">How a release moves through the system</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Every command is a pure function returning a plan, plus a separate step that touches disk &mdash; modeled explicitly on release-please's compute/apply split.</p>

      <div class="diagram-frame">
        <p class="fig-label">Fig. 2 &mdash; changeset to published package</p>
        <pre class="mermaid">
%%{init: {"theme":"base","themeVariables":{"primaryColor":"#F7F5EF","primaryTextColor":"#211D16","primaryBorderColor":"#CFC9B8","lineColor":"#8B8570","secondaryColor":"#EFDCC7","tertiaryColor":"#DCE9E9","fontFamily":"ui-monospace, SF Mono, Menlo, monospace","fontSize":"13px"}}}%%
flowchart LR
    CS["changeset files (.changeset/*.md)"] --> GRAPH["dependency graph (petgraph)"]
    GRAPH --> SCC["cycle check (Tarjan SCC)"]
    SCC --> CASCADE["cascade bumps (topo order)"]
    CASCADE --> APPLY["apply (CST edit + atomic write)"]
    APPLY --> CHLOG["changelog + tag"]
    CHLOG --> PUB["publish (cargo / npm / twine)"]

    classDef step fill:#F7F5EF,stroke:#CFC9B8,color:#211D16
    class CS,GRAPH,SCC,CASCADE,APPLY,CHLOG,PUB step
    linkStyle default stroke:#8B8570,stroke-width:1.5px
        </pre>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">The cascade step is where the real decisions happen: for each dependency edge &mdash; Runtime, Dev, Peer, Optional, Build &mdash; it checks whether the dependent's existing semver range already <em>covers</em> the new version, and only escalates a bump when it doesn't. A Peer edge that falls outside its declared range can be configured to escalate all the way to a major bump.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="plate" id="specimens">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate VI</p><h2 class="plate-title">Feature specimens</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Eight systems, each solving one specific failure mode observed in existing tools.</p>

      <div class="specimen-grid">
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">CST-preserving edits</span><span class="sp-tag">manifests</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">Every manifest write goes through <code>toml_edit</code>'s concrete syntax tree or a JSON indentation-fingerprinting pass &mdash; never regex, never destructive re-serialization. Comments, key order, and whitespace survive every bump.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">Atomic, crash-safe writes</span><span class="sp-tag">persistence</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">Temp file in the same directory &rarr; <code>fsync</code> &rarr; atomic rename &rarr; <code>fsync</code> the parent and grandparent directories. A killed process never leaves a half-written manifest. Gated by a capability token, not a code-review reminder.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">Topological cascade engine</span><span class="sp-tag">graph</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">A <code>petgraph</code> DAG runs Tarjan's SCC before any bump math &mdash; circular dependencies get caught before they ship, not discovered in production.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">Version groups</span><span class="sp-tag">fixed / linked</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">Fixed groups lock-step to the max computed bump; linked groups share severity but keep independent offsets. A napi crate's group is seeded once from <code>napi.targets</code>, then owned by config &mdash; never silently re-derived.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">callisto matrix</span><span class="sp-tag">CI discovery</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">Reads napi-rs and maturin native build targets straight from manifests and emits a CI matrix as JSON &mdash; one source of truth instead of hand-maintained, drift-prone YAML arrays.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">Pre-release cycles</span><span class="sp-tag">pre.json</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">Byte-compatible with changesets' <code>pre.json</code>. <code>pre.0 &rarr; pre.1 &rarr; pre.2</code> stays monotonic and idempotent by computing from a snapshot of initial versions, not on-disk state.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">Snapshot releases</span><span class="sp-tag">transient</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">A throwaway version like <code>0.0.0-snapshot-&lt;sha&gt;</code>, written uncommitted and untagged. Callisto never publishes it itself &mdash; that's deliberately the calling CI workflow's job.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">Publish coordinator, not reimplementor</span><span class="sp-tag">registries</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">Shells out to <code>cargo publish</code>, <code>npm publish</code>, <code>twine upload</code> &mdash; never talks to a registry HTTP API directly. Callisto's job is deciding what and when, not re-solving auth for three ecosystems.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="specimen">
          <div class="sp-head"><span class="sp-name">Native speed</span><span class="sp-tag">performance</span></div>
          <div class="sp-body">Workspace discovery and version planning run in under 10ms &mdash; a native Rust binary against a Node.js runtime, not a close race. README's own claim: 30&ndash;50&times; faster than <code>@changesets/cli</code> or <code>release-please</code>.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="plate" id="principles">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate VII</p><h2 class="plate-title">Design principles</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Seven rules, formally enumerated in the design doc, that every feature above answers to.</p>

      <div class="principle-list">
        <div class="principle">
          <div class="p-id">P1</div>
          <div><div class="p-name">Byte-compatibility is the adoption gate</div><div class="p-body">Matching changesets' file format exactly is a hard requirement &mdash; nothing overrides it. A team can adopt in one commit and roll back in one commit.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <div class="p-id">P2</div>
          <div><div class="p-name">Statelessness for correctness</div><div class="p-body">Any state file Callisto writes is gitignored and never load-bearing for a correctness decision.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <div class="p-id">P3</div>
          <div><div class="p-name">Idempotence per phase</div><div class="p-body">Re-running a command against unchanged state produces the same result, not a compounding one.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <div class="p-id">P4</div>
          <div><div class="p-name">Polyglot uniformity via trait boundaries</div><div class="p-body">A grammar and a workspace concept per ecosystem &mdash; not just a manifest format per ecosystem.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <div class="p-id">P5</div>
          <div><div class="p-name">Structural fixes over discipline</div><div class="p-body">A failure class gets fixed in code &mdash; a check, an invariant, a refusal &mdash; never just documented as something to remember.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <div class="p-id">P6</div>
          <div><div class="p-name">Release semantics live in the core</div><div class="p-body">Every wrapper &mdash; the Moon plugin, a GitHub Action &mdash; is a thin dispatcher. None of them compute bumps, cascades, publish order, or changelogs themselves.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <div class="p-id">P7</div>
          <div><div class="p-name">Every boundary is a versioned contract</div><div class="p-body">Core-to-integration boundaries are explicit, versioned, fixtured JSON/API contracts &mdash; not an informal understanding.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="plate" id="cli">
      <div class="plate-head"><p class="plate-num">Plate VIII</p><h2 class="plate-title">Command surface</h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Fourteen subcommands, one binary. Every one supports <code>--format json</code> for scripting and <code>--dry-run</code> where it mutates anything.</p>

